statement:
Each function below compiles without an error. **Select every one whose
return value is dangling** — that is, every one where the caller is handed a
reference or a pointer to storage whose lifetime has already ended.

```cpp
// A
const std::string& first_word(const std::string& line)
{
    static const std::string empty{};
    auto at = line.find(' ');
    return at == std::string::npos ? line : empty;
}

// B
const std::string& longest(const std::string& a, const std::string& b)
{
    return a.size() >= b.size() ? a : b;
}

// C
const char* label(int n)
{
    std::string s = "n = " + std::to_string(n);
    return s.c_str();
}

// D
std::string_view trimmed(const std::string& s)
{
    return std::string_view{ s }.substr(1);
}
```

Assume every caller passes a named `std::string` variable that outlives the
call, and uses the result immediately.
